# Line-ending policy: LF in the repository and LF on checkout, on every platform.
#
# Without this file, checkout endings depend on each developer's core.autocrlf.
# With autocrlf=true (the Git-for-Windows default) a Windows checkout produces
# CRLF files, and `prettier --check` — whose endOfLine default is "lf" — rejects
# them. Linux CI still passes, so the result is a check that fails only on some
# machines. That is worse than failing everywhere.
#
# It also protects the shell scripts: a CRLF `.sh` fails on Linux with
# "bash: \r: command not found", which is a confusing way to break CI.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the files where CRLF is actually fatal.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf

# Binary, so they are never line-ending converted or diffed as text.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary

# Generated: keep them out of diffs and reviews, but still track them.
frontend/package-lock.json  -diff linguist-generated
backend/uv.lock             -diff linguist-generated
